{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS003725","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are abundant (85) and many have strong phenotype alignment to coronary artery disease, but the evidence is heterogeneous (incident vs prevalent vs recurrent events; some special-population endpoints; many report only full-model AUROC with unknown or clinically augmented covariates), which limits a single clearly dominant choice.

PGS003725 is selected as the best-supported direct-match candidate because: (1) phenotype alignment is strong (trait_reported and phenotyping_reported are both “Coronary artery disease”); (2) method is a genome-wide shrinkage approach (LDpred2) with a high variant count (1,296,172), a favorable structural signal versus sparse GWAS-hit scores; (3) it is explicitly positioned as a multi-ancestry CAD PRS with broad evaluation across multiple ancestries (AFR/AMR/EAS/EUR/MAE/SAS), supporting transportability context; and (4) it reports strong per-SD effect sizes (HR=1.75; OR=2.14), which is supportive where PRS-only AUC/R2 are not provided.

Main limitation: PGS003725 lacks PRS-comparable discrimination metrics (no PGS-only AUROC/R2), and several competing CAD scores also have strong endpoints and/or reported full-model AUROCs (e.g., PGS000018 AUROC=0.89; PGS001780 AUROC=0.913) but those are full-model metrics and potentially covariate-driven/non-comparable; without consistent PRS-only metrics across the leading candidates, confidence in a single “best” model remains moderate rather than high."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004860",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for lung cancer are present (multiple models with trait_reported/phenotyping_reported consistent with lung cancer, including incident lung cancer and lung cancer case definitions). Among them, PGS004860 is best supported by the visible evidence because it is phenotype-aligned (phenotyping_reported: Incident lung cancer; trait_efo: lung carcinoma), uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (LDpred2) with very large variant count (1,143,554), and reports a strong full-model AUROC with CI (0.893, 0.887–0.898) plus an effect size (OR 1.33). The main limitation is that performance reported is full-model AUROC (with covariates including smoking status and BMI) and no PGS-only AUROC/R2 is provided, making cross-model PRS-specific comparison uncertain; additionally, other direct-match candidates (e.g., PGS000078/PGS000789) also show high full-model AUROC but with heavier covariate packages, and many candidates lack PRS-only metrics, so the evidence does not cleanly identify a single unequivocal winner."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004760",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates are present (multiple models with trait_efo/phenotyping_reported aligned to depressive disorder / major depressive disorder). Among them, PGS004760 is best supported in the provided metadata because it targets depression/MDD (trait_efo: major depressive disorder; phenotyping_reported: Depression), uses a multi-component/ensemble-style method (PRSmixPlus; a favorable method family per the domain guidance vs clumping/thresholding or small GWAS-hit scores), and reports an explicit PRS contribution metric (incremental R2=0.024 with CI 0.018–0.03) with basic covariates (age, sex, PCs). Main limitation: many competing direct-match models also have strong endpoint alignment and some report higher-looking metrics that are not clearly PRS-only/comparable to incremental R2 (e.g., full-model AUROC/C-index or context-specific endpoints like antidepressant side effects, imaging traits, or incident-only designs), and there is no uniform PRS-only AUC/R2 across the pool, so the selection is evidence-limited rather than definitive."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002082","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All listed candidates are direct matches to the target lupus erythematosus concept (either the umbrella EFO term "lupus erythematosus" or the closely aligned diagnosis endpoint "systemic lupus erythematosus"). Several models, however, have endpoint narrowing (e.g., lupus nephritis/class V nephritis, renal disease, early-onset only, arthritis outpatient ascertainment, or "erythematous conditions"), which reduces endpoint fidelity for generic lupus erythematosus risk.

Among the two exact-label umbrella candidates, PGS002082 ("Lupus (localized and systemic)") is preferred over PGS001870 because it uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (LDpred2) with a very large variant count (361,553 vs 87), and it reports a slightly higher PRS-comparable metric (partial-r 0.0212 vs 0.0184) in the same portability study context and with the same phenotype/covariate framing.

Main limitation: the strongest-looking AUROCs in the pool (e.g., PGS003960 AUROC 0.89) are full-model metrics with non-comparable covariate augmentation (phenotype risk score), and for PGS002082 the available performance evidence is limited to a small partial correlation from a UKB portability framework rather than clear PGS-only AUROC/R2 on a clinical SLE diagnosis endpoint. This makes the direct-match recommendation only moderately supported overall."}
